{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization experiment is set to end on Sunday as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers individuals caught with illicit substances the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe law aims to make it easier for law enforcement to tackle public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs in areas such as parks. Personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail. While the law encourages counties to develop treatment alternatives, it does not make them mandatory, potentially creating inconsistent policies across the state.\n\n*** \nSupporters argue that this flexibility allows counties to tailor programs to their resources, but opponents fear it may lead to unequal treatment. To be eligible for deflection, individuals must meet strict criteria, including having no outstanding warrants and being medically stable. If they qualify, officers will connect them to a mobile behavioral health team within 30 minutes.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, it's worth considering the broader implications for the country. As people navigate this new landscape, they may need to be mindful of their surroundings, much like when they're out shopping or deciding what to eat. It's a complex issue that can leave one tongue-tied, particularly when considering the impact on different age groups. \nP.P.S As the country grapples with these changes, it's clear that the conversation around drug policy is far from over, and it's an issue that will continue to be on the tongue of many, affecting people of all age groups.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.17907345